{
"dur": 59.329,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 5.036,
"text": "In 1999, Nvidia went public, and launched the GeForce 256.",
"end": 5.311
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 5.311,
"vo_start": 5.586,
"vo_end": 10.746,
"text": "It marketed the chip with a new name: the GPU, the graphics processing unit.",
"end": 11.021
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 11.021,
"vo_start": 11.296,
"vo_end": 19.416,
"text": "The GeForce took over 3D calculations that used to fall on the computer's main processor. Games got smoother, lighting got better, worlds got bigger.",
"end": 19.691
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 19.691,
"vo_start": 19.966,
"vo_end": 27.766,
"text": "By the early 2000s, Nvidia was the leading name in PC graphics. Gamers, game studios and film effects houses all ran on it.",
"end": 28.041
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 28.041,
"vo_start": 28.316,
"vo_end": 33.798,
"text": "Nvidia also won the deal to supply the graphics chip for Microsoft's first Xbox in 2001.",
"end": 34.073
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 34.073,
"vo_start": 34.348,
"vo_end": 42.679,
"text": "Nvidia won partly through speed. Jensen pushed the team to ship a new generation of chips roughly every six months, while rivals took a year or more.",
"end": 42.954
},
{
"i": 7,
"start": 42.954,
"vo_start": 43.229,
"vo_end": 48.029,
"text": "Its biggest rival, 3dfx, the maker of the Voodoo cards that gamers had loved, couldn't keep up.",
"end": 48.304
},
{
"i": 8,
"start": 48.304,
"vo_start": 48.579,
"vo_end": 58.379,
"text": "At the end of 2000, Nvidia bought what was left of 3dfx. Five years after the NV1 flop, Nvidia was the company others were trying to catch.",
"end": 59.329
}
],
